  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td width=\"30px\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"40px\" align=\"center\">${e}^{2}$</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2{e}^{2}$</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"right\">${e}^{2({J}_{4}(1))}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{2}$</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2{e}^{2}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{2}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2{e}^{2}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
  "worked_solution": "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ $n=4,$ and $\\lambda =1.$\n\nSince $n=4,$ we need to compute the first $4-1=3$ derivatives of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =8{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{4}(1)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(1) & \\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2\\cdot 1} & 2{e}^{2\\cdot 1} & \\frac{4{e}^{2\\cdot 1}}{2} & \\frac{8{e}^{2\\cdot 1}}{6} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2\\cdot 1} & 2{e}^{2\\cdot 1} & \\frac{4{e}^{2\\cdot 1}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{2\\cdot 1} & 2{e}^{2\\cdot 1} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & {e}^{2\\cdot 1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & 2{e}^{2} & 2{e}^{2} & \\frac{4{e}^{2}}{3} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2} & 2{e}^{2} & 2{e}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{2} & 2{e}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & {e}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
